WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.411
Checks and balances, from the first lesson.

2
00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:08.067
You just wrote a test that compares an analytic gradient against a finite-difference estimate.

3
00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:12.251
Hold onto that habit; it is the most transferable thing in this lesson.

4
00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:25.373
When you later write a custom loss, a custom layer, or a fine-tuning script that does anything nonstandard, a gradient check on a tiny input is the fastest way to know whether the math in your code is the math in your head.

5
00:00:25.653 --> 00:00:27.659
Two practical notes on doing it well.

6
00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:42.296
Use the central difference with h around 1e-5 to 1e-6 in double precision, and compare with a relative error (the absolute difference divided by the sum of magnitudes), because gradients span many orders of magnitude.

7
00:00:42.576 --> 00:00:45.648
And check on small, random, non-symmetric inputs.

8
00:00:45.928 --> 00:00:52.819
Symmetric inputs such as all-ones can hide a swapped index, because the wrong entry happens to have the right value.

9
00:00:53.099 --> 00:01:02.208
The same idea, testing a component against an independent method rather than against your expectations, is what Studio 3 calls evaluation.

10
00:01:02.488 --> 00:01:07.885
Here the independent method is calculus; there it is labeled data or a second grader.
